  "a3b-s12": {
    id: "a3b-s12", name: "A national identity after independence", intro: "Use the passage below to answer the questions that follow.",
    sources: [{ title: "Source", blocks: [
      { p: "After independence, Americans worked to create a national identity to match their new government. Printers sold almanacs, spelling books, and histories that praised the Revolution; towns marked the Fourth of July with parades and toasts; and George Washington became a national hero whose image hung in homes from New Hampshire to Georgia. Yet this new identity was narrower and weaker than its celebrations suggested. Most people still felt their first loyalty to their state or local community, and travel and news moved slowly between regions. Political leaders also defined the nation's people largely as free white men. Women, free and enslaved African Americans, and Native nations took part in public life, but they were usually left out of the picture that celebrations of the new nation painted." },
      { cite: "Practice passage written for this review, summarizing an interpretation held by many historians.", kind: "practice" },
    ] }],
  },
