  "a3b-s17": {
    id: "a3b-s17", name: "How much did the Revolution change by 1800?", intro: "Use the passage below to answer the questions that follow.",
    sources: [{ title: "Source", blocks: [
      { p: "Historians disagree about how much the American Revolution changed American society by 1800. In one view, change was real but limited. Most states still required voters to own property or pay taxes, women could not vote or hold office in most places, and the Constitution protected slavery through the three-fifths clause and a twenty-year guarantee for the slave trade. Native nations lost land faster once Britain no longer held back settlers. Yet the Revolution also set loose ideas that later generations used. Northern states began to end slavery, several states ended their tax-supported churches, and the Declaration's promise of equality became a standard that excluded groups could hold the nation to. The Revolution's results, in this view, depended less on what it achieved by 1800 than on how its language was used afterward." },
      { cite: "Practice passage written for this review, summarizing an interpretation held by many historians.", kind: "practice" },
    ] }],
  },

  // ---------- free response ----------
  "a3b-s20": {
    id: "a3b-s20", name: "Why the Constitution was written: two views", intro: "Use the passages below to answer all parts of the question that follows.",
    sources: [
      { title: "Source 1", blocks: [
        { p: "The Constitution was above all a victory for men of property. Merchants, holders of government bonds, and large landowners had suffered under the Confederation: Congress could not pay its debts, several states printed paper money, and legislatures passed laws that shielded debtors from their creditors. The men who met at Philadelphia came largely from this propertied class, and they built a government that could tax, pay the public debt, and stop the states from interfering with contracts. The struggle over ratification pitted these commercial interests, strongest in the port towns, against small farmers in the backcountry, who saw the new system as a scheme of the wealthy. The Constitution, in this view, was a conservative reaction that limited the democratic gains of the Revolution." },
        { cite: "Practice passage written for this review, summarizing an interpretation held by many historians.", kind: "practice" },
      ] },
      { title: "Source 2", blocks: [
        { p: "The framers were driven less by their own pocketbooks than by alarm about the survival of the republic. Under the Articles, the Union could not defend its western frontier, force Britain to leave its forts, or win respect from Spain on the Mississippi. Shays' Rebellion seemed to show that the states could not keep order. Delegates differed widely in their personal economic interests, and they argued mostly about representation, executive power, and the balance between the states and the nation. The Constitution they produced reflected the political lessons of a decade of experiments with self-government, especially the fear that power concentrated anywhere, including in popular state legislatures, threatened liberty." },
        { cite: "Practice passage written for this review, summarizing an interpretation held by many historians.", kind: "practice" },
      ] },
    ],
  },
